{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-83679",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/8c3c9e40f56d0b269562c37fd2d87c7e8f0394c8f55dca596e5e751f5e96157b.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the kite, and $z$ is the distance between the kite and Neah. The quantity $z=112\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the kite string's length is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{112} \\\\ y & =112\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(112\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(112\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{28}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(112\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\pi }{28} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the kite is gaining altitude is $4\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}.$",
